const enBody = `
<h1 id="top">Gemini Omni 1.2 Flash: $0.07/sec Video API Cost</h1>

<p>On November 5, 2026, Google made Gemini Omni 1.2 Flash generally available on the Gemini API and Google Cloud. The release does three things that matter to an API buyer: native clip length goes from 10 seconds to 15, video output gets multi-turn conversational editing, and the output rate drops 30 percent to $12.00 per 1M video tokens. That last number works out to about $0.07 per second of 720p output, and it is the headline every pricing page repeats.</p>

<p>Here is the thing the headline leaves out. $0.07 is one cell in a four-row table, and the table is not linear. The same model costs $0.023 per second at 360p, $0.104 at 1080p, and $0.209 at 4K. Worse, upscaling is billed at its own token rate, and it is the rate people get wrong most often, because a 1080p clip rendered from a 720p draft is not the same price as a native 1080p clip. Get that wrong across a batch of a thousand product videos and the difference is thousands of dollars.</p>

<p>This review walks through the actual billing mechanics, the API shape, the hard limits, and where the model does not work. Everything quoted here was pulled from Google's live documentation and pricing pages on November 6, 2026, and the per-second figures are recomputed from Google's published token rates rather than copied from a blog post.</p>

<h2 id="shipped">What Google shipped on November 5</h2>

<p>Omni 1.2 Flash is a general-availability release, not a preview. The model code is <code>gemini-omni-1.2-flash</code>, it is available on the paid tier of the Gemini API, and it is also listed on Vertex AI at identical rates: $1.50 per 1M input tokens, $9.00 per 1M text output tokens, and $12.00 per 1M video output tokens. Provisioned Throughput is available for teams that need reserved capacity, and outputs carry an imperceptible SynthID watermark.</p>

<p>The feature set that separates 1.2 from 1.1 is not just length. Reference adherence improved, meaning a character's face, a garment's cut, packaging typography, and brand colors survive the whole clip rather than drifting by the final frame. Multi-turn conversational editing works across a full 15-second clip, which is the part that changes how you build: a background swap or an aspect-ratio change is a follow-up turn, not a fresh generation that throws away the take you already paid for. Google also added native audio with clearer lip-sync and distinct multi-speaker conditioning.</p>

<p>Clip duration is set in <code>response_format.duration</code> as a whole second from 3 to 15. The default is 10 seconds. If you set the duration explicitly, it overrides any length mentioned in the prompt text, and asking for more than 15 seconds returns an invalid argument error rather than silently truncating.</p>

<h2 id="ladder">The price ladder, recomputed</h2>

<p>Google bills video output by total output token consumption. The per-second figure is a derived convenience, not a rate you are actually charged. The underlying arithmetic uses four published constants: 1,931 tokens per second of 360p output, 5,792 tokens per second of 720p, 8,688 tokens per second of 1080p, and 17,376 tokens per second of 4K. Multiply by seconds, multiply by $12.00 per 1M, and you get the effective rate.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Output resolution</th><th>Video tokens / second</th><th>Effective cost per second</th><th>10-second clip</th><th>15-second clip</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>360p</td><td>1,931</td><td>$0.023</td><td>$0.23</td><td>$0.35</td></tr>
<tr><td>720p</td><td>5,792</td><td>$0.070</td><td>$0.70</td><td>$1.04</td></tr>
<tr><td>1080p</td><td>8,688</td><td>$0.104</td><td>$1.04</td><td>$1.56</td></tr>
<tr><td>4K</td><td>17,376</td><td>$0.209</td><td>$2.09</td><td>$3.13</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Those figures reproduce Google's published numbers exactly, which is the check that matters: 5,792 tokens times 10 seconds times $12.00 per 1M equals $0.695 for a 10-second 720p clip, matching the roughly $0.70 Google states. The 15-second 720p clip lands at $1.04, matching Google's $1.04. If a source quotes a per-second price that does not fall out of these four constants and the $12.00 rate, that source is wrong.</p>

<p>The 30 percent cut lands where you would expect. Omni 1.1 Flash charged $17.50 per 1M video output tokens, which is $0.101 per second of 720p. Dropping to $12.00 is a 31.4 percent reduction on the video line, and Google's "30 percent" framing is accurate within rounding. Note that the input rate and the text output rate did not move: input stayed at $1.50 per 1M and text output at $9.00 per 1M. If your workload is mostly text and thinking tokens with a little video, the saving is smaller than the headline suggests.</p>

<h2 id="upscale">The upscaling trap</h2>

<p>This is where the token math stops being intuitive. Output above 720p is produced by upscaling, and upscaling carries its own token rate that differs from the native rate at the same resolution. Google publishes these separately: 2,896 tokens per second for 360p to 720p, 2,896 for 720p to 1080p, 5,792 for 360p to 1080p, and 11,584 for both 720p to 4K and 1080p to 4K.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Render path</th><th>Total tokens / second</th><th>Effective cost per second</th><th>10-second clip</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>360p native</td><td>1,931</td><td>$0.023</td><td>$0.23</td></tr>
<tr><td>360p to 720p</td><td>4,827</td><td>$0.058</td><td>$0.58</td></tr>
<tr><td>720p native</td><td>5,792</td><td>$0.070</td><td>$0.70</td></tr>
<tr><td>720p to 1080p</td><td>8,688</td><td>$0.104</td><td>$1.04</td></tr>
<tr><td>1080p native</td><td>8,688</td><td>$0.104</td><td>$1.04</td></tr>
<tr><td>720p to 4K</td><td>17,376</td><td>$0.209</td><td>$2.09</td></tr>
<tr><td>1080p to 4K</td><td>20,272</td><td>$0.243</td><td>$2.43</td></tr>
<tr><td>4K native</td><td>17,376</td><td>$0.209</td><td>$2.09</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two results deserve a second look. First, rendering 360p and upscaling to 720p costs 4,827 tokens per second, about 17 percent less than rendering 720p natively. That is the opposite of the usual assumption that higher native resolution is the expensive path, and it happens because the upscale factor at that step is cheaper per token than native 720p generation. Second, going 1080p to 4K costs 20,272 tokens per second, about 17 percent more than rendering 4K directly at 17,376. Rendering 4K natively and downscaling is cheaper than the obvious upscale route.</p>

<p>Neither observation is a recommendation, because both ignore quality. A 360p draft upscaled to 720p is not the same asset as a native 720p render, and the 4K-from-4K path may well produce a cleaner result than 4K-from-1080p. What the arithmetic tells you is that "draft low, upscale high" is not automatically the cheap strategy the workflow suggests. The cost-optimal version of that workflow is to pick the two resolutions where the upscale happens to be favorable, and to verify the quality difference on your own footage before committing a budget to it.</p>

<p>A concrete batch example makes it concrete. Three 10-second 360p drafts plus one 10-second 1080p final runs about $1.74. Three 10-second 1080p finals run about $3.13. If you are shipping a hundred variants of one ad, that gap is roughly $140 per hundred, which is the kind of number that decides whether the pipeline is viable.</p>

<h2 id="api">The API: one endpoint, no polling</h2>

<p>Video generation runs through Google's Interactions API, not the older <code>generateContent</code> path. It is a single synchronous request that returns the finished clip as base64 in the response body. There is no job queue to poll and no callback to wire up, which is a meaningful simplification against providers that return a polling URL.</p>

<pre><code>curl -X POST "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/interactions?key=$GEMINI_API_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "model": "gemini-omni-1.2-flash",
    "input": "A single unbroken tracking shot following a paper airplane through a library.",
    "response_format": {
      "type": "video",
      "duration": "15s",
      "resolution": "1080p",
      "aspect_ratio": "9:16"
    }
  }'</code></pre>

<p>The SDK path is the same shape with the response handling written for you:</p>

<pre><code>import base64
from google import genai

client = genai.Client()
interaction = client.interactions.create(
    model="gemini-omni-1.2-flash",
    input="A single unbroken tracking shot following a paper airplane through a library.",
    response_format={
        "type": "video",
        "duration": "15s",
        "resolution": "1080p",
        "aspect_ratio": "9:16",
    },
)
with open("clip.mp4", "wb") as f:
    f.write(base64.b64decode(interaction.output_video.data))</code></pre>

<p>Two details will trip up a first integration. The <code>output_video</code> convenience field is SDK-only, so a direct REST caller has to walk the <code>steps</code> array and pull the <code>model_output</code> entry, whose content item carries <code>mime_type: video/mp4</code> and a base64 <code>data</code> field. And the <code>aspect_ratio</code> parameter accepts only <code>9:16</code> and <code>16:9</code>, with landscape as the default.</p>

<p>The multi-turn editing loop is where the cost model changes. A follow-up turn that swaps the background or changes the lighting is billed as another generation, not as a cheap edit operation. That makes the conversational workflow a cost multiplier as much as a time saver, and it is the strongest argument for the draft-at-360p approach: iterate at $0.023 per second, then commit to a 720p or 1080p final once the take is right.</p>

<h2 id="limits">Limits you will hit</h2>

<p>The rate limits are the first wall. There is no free tier at all: the pricing page lists both the input and the output price for this model as not available on the free tier, so you cannot prototype on a cardless account the way you can with the rest of the Gemini catalog. Paid tiers start at 8 requests per minute and 40 per day on Tier 1, 16 per minute and 200 per day on Tier 2, and 40 per minute with 2,000 per day on Tier 3. Priority inference runs at 0.3 times the standard limit.</p>

<p>Those request limits are per clip, not per second. A 15-second 720p clip is one request, so a Tier 1 account can generate at most 40 clips a day and 8 a minute, roughly $28 of 720p video per day at Tier 1. Batch workloads need a higher tier.</p>

<p>Duration limits are more subtle. Interpolation between a first and last frame works from 3 to 10 seconds only, and passing both frames with a duration over 10 seconds returns an invalid argument error rather than falling back to single-image generation. Extension works from 3 to 10 seconds per turn, up to 30 seconds of total video, and uses only the last 10 seconds of the input as context. If a character or a product only appears before that 10-second lookback window, you have to mention it again in the prompt or pass it as a reference image.</p>

<p>Reference material has its own budget: up to 6 reference images across all durations, and clips from 11 to 15 seconds accept only 1 reference video of up to 3 seconds, while clips from 3 to 10 seconds accept up to 3. Extension is end-of-clip only, so you cannot prepend content or extend the middle of a clip. And extending uploaded videos is not currently available for users in the European Economic Area, Switzerland, and the United Kingdom, though extending videos the model generated is supported in all available regions.</p>

<p>One more constraint is worth flagging for anyone generating speech: you cannot extend an uploaded video of someone talking in order to add more dialogue. Extending a clip where the character is silent, or where the prompt adds no dialogue, does work. Adding dialogue to a previously generated clip works through the multi-turn <code>previous_interaction_id</code> path.</p>

<h2 id="ecosystem">OpenRouter, Vertex, and the resellers</h2>

<p>OpenRouter lists <code>google/gemini-omni-1.2-flash</code> with a 131,072-token context length at $1.50 per 1M input and $9.00 per 1M output, matching Google's direct rates, with the Google AI Studio endpoint as the only route. Vertex AI lists the same $1.50, $9.00, and $12.00 figures with identical token constants, so direct and Vertex pricing are currently the same deal. On both, the bare <code>google/gemini-omni-flash</code> preview model is gone and only the versioned 1.1 and 1.2 IDs remain.</p>

<p>The resellers are where it gets uneven. If you want to compare several video models from one integration, a gen-media aggregator is the practical option: <a href="https://fal.ai/?utm_source=apirank" target="_blank" rel="sponsored noopener">fal.ai</a> runs Gemini Omni alongside Kling 3.0, Seedance 2.5, Wan 3.0, LTX 2.5, and HeyGen behind one request shape, which saves you a client per vendor when you are still deciding which model wins your use case. But check the effective per-second price rather than the model name. As of November 6, 2026, fal.ai's <code>google/gemini-omni-flash</code> listing still showed a token rate of $21.875 per 1M, which computes to roughly $0.125 per second of 720p video, about 80 percent above the direct Google rate. The listing had not been repriced to the 1.2 rate yet.</p>

<p>For most teams the direct Gemini API is the cheaper answer and also the one with the newest features, since multi-turn editing and the 15-second cap are Google-side behaviors that a proxy may lag behind. The aggregator case is strongest when you are still evaluating models side by side and want one integration to rule them all.</p>

<h2 id="migration">The December 3 migration</h2>

<p>Two model IDs are in play and one is quietly gone. <code>gemini-omni-1.1-flash</code> is stable but scheduled to route to <code>gemini-omni-1.2-flash</code> on December 3, 2026, and from that date its requests are billed at the 1.2 rates. The earlier <code>gemini-omni-flash-preview</code> is shut down. Meanwhile the Veo 3.1 Preview family in the Gemini API was retired on October 22, 2026, with <code>veo-3.1-generate-preview</code>, <code>veo-3.1-fast-generate-preview</code>, and <code>veo-3.1-lite-generate-preview</code> all gone, and Google's own deprecations table naming <code>gemini-omni-1.2-flash</code> as the recommended replacement for each.</p>

<p>That Veo shutdown is the part worth acting on. If you had integrated Veo 3.1 in the Gemini API, you have already migrated or you are broken. If you had priced against Veo 3.1 in a planning doc, that number is stale: the replacement costs $0.07 per second at 720p rather than the $0.10 to $0.40 per second band Veo used to occupy. Google also moved Veo 3.1 GA to the Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, which is a separate billing relationship from the AI Studio API.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">Who should use it, and who should not</h2>

<p><strong>Use Omni 1.2 Flash if</strong> you are shipping short product or social video at volume and want a single API for it. The $0.07 per second at 720p is genuinely competitive, native audio removes a separate audio model from the pipeline, multi-turn editing stops you re-briefing from scratch on every revision, and the 15-second ceiling covers most ad and explainer formats in one generation. The billing is token-based but fully predictable, because the token constants per resolution are published rather than estimated.</p>

<p><strong>Use it carefully if</strong> your output needs 1080p or 4K, because that is where the cost curve bends. A 15-second 4K clip runs about $3.13, and a 4K clip rendered from a 1080p source runs about $2.43. If you need 4K for a large share of your volume, model the per-clip cost before you scale, and consider whether a 1080p deliverable is acceptable at 4K-adjacent bitrates. This is also where the multi-turn workflow multiplies: every revision at 4K is another $3.13.</p>

<p><strong>Skip it if</strong> you need a free tier for prototyping, or you need clips longer than 30 seconds, or you are generating dialogue-heavy content by extending uploaded footage. There is no cardless path into this model, 30 seconds is the hard ceiling even with extension, and adding speech to an uploaded talking clip is not supported.</p>

<p><strong>Plan around it if</strong> you are on the December 3 migration. Pin <code>gemini-omni-1.2-flash</code> explicitly rather than relying on <code>gemini-omni-1.1-flash</code>, because the older ID will silently route to the new model and start billing at the new rates. If you pinned an alias that resolved to the preview model, that alias is already dead. Model IDs are cheap to pin and expensive to leave implicit.</p>

<p>The verdict is that Omni 1.2 Flash is the cheapest serious native-video price Google has shipped, and the engineering around it is unusually well documented. The pricing model is transparent enough that you can compute your exact monthly bill from a spreadsheet, which is rarer than it should be. The costs that will surprise you are not the headline rate but the upscale paths and the rate limits, and both are knowable in advance.</p>

<p><strong>Sources:</strong> Google, "Gemini Omni 1.2 Flash: Fast and cost-effective with longer clips" (Nov 5, 2026); Google AI for Developers, Gemini API pricing (verified Nov 6, 2026); Gemini API model reference for Gemini Omni Flash (verified Nov 6, 2026); Gemini API video generation and Gemini Omni documentation guides (verified Nov 6, 2026); Gemini API rate limits and deprecations pages (verified Nov 6, 2026); Google Cloud Vertex AI generative AI pricing (verified Nov 6, 2026); OpenRouter model catalog and endpoint list (verified Nov 6, 2026); fal.ai model catalog and Gemini Omni Flash listing (verified Nov 6, 2026).</p>
`;
